   the package (Table 230, p.346): LQFP100 junction to ambient 45.0 C/W (PRINTED); maximum junction 125 C (Table 23, p.105; VOS0: 105 C);
   suffix 6 ambient to 85 C at maximum dissipation; VDD 1.62 to 3.6 V (PRINTED)
   rv-pwr's HIGH, 400 mA, is rev Y's 400 MHz row with all peripherals enabled AT TJ 85 C; the same row prints 840 mA at TJ 125 C

4. THE JUNCTION TEMPERATURE at L4-E12's inside air, 76.25 C (E5's dwell, MODELED; 81.89 C in the exhaust; inside the part's 85 C ambient)
   a controller dissipates VDD x IDD (its own regulator is linear): at that air its junction reaches 125 C at 0.328 A. Above that current the
   H743 ITSELF is outside its rating, whatever regulator feeds it. Each state's operating point TJ = air + 45.0 C/W x 3.3 V x Imax(TJ), the
   maxima interpolated between their printed columns (MODEL on PRINTED figures):
   Y    VOS3  25 MHz, peripherals disabled  TJ 95.9 C at 0.1322 A
   Y    VOS3  60 MHz, peripherals disabled  TJ 97.1 C at 0.1402 A
   Y    VOS3 144 MHz, peripherals disabled  TJ 101.4 C at 0.1691 A
   Y    VOS3 200 MHz, peripherals disabled  TJ 104.0 C at 0.1871 A
   Y    VOS3 200 MHz, peripherals enabled   NO OPERATING POINT at or under 125 C: the controller is outside its rating at this air in this state
   Y    VOS1 400 MHz, peripherals disabled  NO OPERATING POINT at or under 125 C: the controller is outside its rating at this air in this state
   Y    VOS1 400 MHz, peripherals enabled   NO OPERATING POINT at or under 125 C: the controller is outside its rating at this air in this state
   V    VOS3  25 MHz, peripherals disabled  TJ 90.3 C at 0.0943 A
   V    VOS3  60 MHz, peripherals disabled  TJ 92.2 C at 0.1075 A
   V    VOS3 144 MHz, peripherals disabled  TJ 96.0 C at 0.1333 A
   V    VOS3 200 MHz, peripherals disabled  TJ 98.5 C at 0.1501 A
   V    VOS3 200 MHz, peripherals enabled   TJ 119.4 C at 0.2906 A
   V    VOS1 400 MHz, peripherals disabled  NO OPERATING POINT at or under 125 C: the controller is outside its rating at this air in this state
   V    VOS1 400 MHz, peripherals enabled   NO OPERATING POINT at or under 125 C: the controller is outside its rating at this air in this state
   V    VOS0 480 MHz, peripherals enabled   NO OPERATING POINT at or under 105 C: the controller is outside its rating at this air in this state
   so at the hot stop's air the worst state the sheet prints is not a state the controller can hold; what bounds it must be the firmware's
   configuration, and no document bounds that (section 2)

5. THE AUXILIARIES' AND THE PERIPHERALS' SHARE
   DECLARED: 0.060 A a supervisor for its three other parts (two TCAN334D transceivers and the SWD and LED branch), in rv-pwr and in the generator
   PRINTED (TI TCAN334, SLLSEQ7F 5.5): ICC recessive 3.5 mA maximum; dominant 55 mA maximum at 60 Ohm (60 mA at 50 Ohm); dominant with a bus
   fault 180 mA maximum. Two transceivers: 7.0 mA recessive, 120 mA while both drive dominant bits, 360 mA with both buses faulted
   so the declared 60 mA covers one transceiver dominant; both dominant at once is a momentary 120 mA (a frame's dominant bits), and the bus
   fault row is a fault state. The controller's own peripherals (two FDCAN, I2C1, the ports, the watchdog) are in neither printed row: the
   'disabled' rows carry none and the 'enabled' rows carry all; their sum for the enabled set is OWED to the contract row (T10-A1)

6. THE DEMAND ON ONE REGULATOR, AS A RANGE
   declared typical (the generator, PLAN-like)                         0.1200 A  DECLARED
   BOUNDED: VOS3, HCLK at most 144 MHz, peripherals as section 5 owes: the controller 0.1691 A at its operating point (TJ 101.4 C; the larger
     revision), plus the declared auxiliaries                          0.2291 A  MODEL on PRINTED maxima and DECLARED auxiliaries; 0.3091 A while both
     transceivers drive dominant bits (momentary)
   the case's HIGH (rv-pwr; C-DEV rev 1's supervisors)                 0.4600 A  PRINTED row at TJ 85 C plus DECLARED auxiliaries; no operating point
     at this air (section 4)
   THE WORST NOTHING FORBIDS: rev Y at 400 MHz, all peripherals, the TJ 125 C column: 0.9000 A (0.9800 A with both transceivers dominant);
     no operating point at this air: the controller overheats first
   so the demand is 0.2291 A where a contract bounds the state, and anything up to 0.9000 A where none does

7. THE REGULATOR AS DRAWN: AP2112K-3.3 (Diodes DS39724 Rev. 2 - 2), one a supervisor, fed from +5V_IOC (I-03's draft: 4.8719 to 5.1329 V)
   PRINTED: output current 600 mA minimum capability; dropout 8 / 200 / 400 mV maximum at 10 / 300 / 600 mA (p.8); input 2.5 to 6.0 V; ambient to
   85 C (p.3); SOT25 junction to ambient 184 C/W, no heat sink (p.3); junction 150 C (absolute maximum, p.3); thermal shutdown 160 C (TYPICAL
   behaviour, no maximum trip printed: never an acceptance); foldback short current 50 mA (TYPICAL). It drops its whole input to 3.3 V: at
   the 5.1329 V maximum, 1.8329 V times its current
   its THERMAL limit at the 76.25 C air: junction 150 C at 0.2187 A; 125 C (this record's criterion, SESSION) at 0.1445 A: it binds far under the 600 mA
     declared typical           0.1200 A: junction  116.7 C: inside the 125 C criterion
     the bounded state          0.2291 A: junction  153.5 C: OVER the absolute maximum
     the declared peak          0.2500 A: junction  160.6 C: OVER the absolute maximum
     the case's HIGH            0.4600 A: junction  231.4 C: OVER the absolute maximum
     the worst nothing forbids  0.9000 A: junction  379.8 C: OVER the absolute maximum; over its 600 mA capability
   FINDING (wider than L9T5-F06 stated it): the deficit is thermal before it is a current limit. As drawn the regulator passes its absolute
   maximum junction at the case's own HIGH (C-DEV rev 1's supervisors), not only in the TJ 125 C state; and with the state unbounded
   nothing keeps the demand under either limit. Moving the LDOs to +5V_IOC (I-03) did not change this; a ground return does not address it

8. THE CORRECTIONS COMPARED (three), THE SELECTION AND ITS ACCEPTANCE CRITERION
   K1 A REGULATOR PER SUPERVISOR that covers the worst: a buck in place of each LDO, the part board B already fits as U25, AP63203WU-7 (Diodes
      DS41326 Rev. 3 - 2: 2 A, input 3.8 to 32 V, TSOT26 89 C/W, junction 160 C absolute; PRINTED). At an efficiency of 0.88 (DECLARED, U25's; its 5 V input
      curve is NOT PLOTTED): junction 85.4 C at the bounded demand and 112.3 C at the worst (MODEL); U601's load 0.5401 A bounded, 2.1218 A at the worst,
      against its 3 A (PRINTED); the lead's pin 1 the same. Changes: board B, three bucks with an inductor, a bootstrap and two output
      capacitors each in the supervisors' pockets, the LDOs' lands and value texts, switching ripple on each controller's VDD and VDDA; board A
      none. Physical: the pockets' placement and routing, the ripple, the start. It does NOT make the unbounded state hold: the controller
      itself has no operating point there (section 4), so K2's row is needed with it as well
   K2 A CONTRACT ROW THAT BOUNDS THE STATE (Layer 5's; its text below), the circuit as drawn: the demand becomes 0.2291 A; the LDO's junction
      153.5 C at this air: OVER the 150 C absolute maximum by 3.5 K, over the 125 C criterion. Changes: none on either board; a firmware
      acceptance and a measured current. Alone it does not hold: the bound is needed and is not enough
   K3 THE SUPERVISORS FED DIFFERENTLY: U601 (board A, I-03's own buck, which feeds nothing else) as a PRE-REGULATOR for the LDOs. Its set point
      is the least on the E96 series, R601 kept, that holds each LDO's input over its requirement at its full 600 mA (3.7693 V: VOUT +1.5 %, load
      regulation 1 %/A, dropout 400 mV; PRINTED) after the rail's 2 % budget, the lead at three times 600 mA and the return's shift AS DRAWN
      (0.0681 V, record l8r2): R602 13.3 k, 4.1805 V nominal, 4.0711 to 4.2907 V; the LDOs' input then at least 3.8428 V
      an LDO drops at most 0.9907 V: junction 125 C at 0.2674 A, 150 C at 0.4046 A (as drawn 0.1445 and 0.2187 A). Junctions: typical 98.1 C, bounded
      118.0 C, the declared peak 121.8 C, the case's HIGH 160.1 C (still over), the worst not covered. Changes: board A, one resistor value and
      the rail's declarations; board B, declarations only (no part, no net); U601's load is the LDOs' own current (1.3800 A at the case's HIGH),
      the lead's pin 1 the same. Physical: the LDOs' real thermal resistance on board B's copper, U601's efficiency at this output, the LDOs'
      accuracy between their dropout and 4.3 V (the sheet tests VOUT at 4.3 V and prints line regulation from 4.3 V; the input here is
      3.8428 to 4.2907 V, inside the printed 2.5 to 6.0 V supply range). A shared 3.3 V buck with no LDO was not taken: it removes each
      controller's own regulator, on which the architecture's failure domains rest
      NOT INSIDE THE BOUND, each row judged against its own limit. Three figures kept apart: 125 C, T10's design criterion (SESSION);
      150 C, the AP2112K's printed absolute maximum junction (DS39724 Rev. 2-2, Absolute Maximum Ratings, p.3), never to be exceeded and never
      an operating target; 160 C, its thermal shutdown, TYPICAL behaviour only (p.8, no maximum trip printed), which shows no protection below
      150 C and is no acceptance. A row over 125 C in a state the design must serve, or over 150 C in any state, FAILS its thermal bound.
      Every junction below is a MODEL figure (the inside air, the printed 184 C/W and the current), never a measured temperature
      (m) both transceivers driving dominant, held: 0.3091 A, junction 132.6 C MODEL with the pre-regulator (180.5 C MODEL as drawn). IN T10's
          SCOPE: normal traffic sets its average. Against 125 C: FAILS at the held figure. A held drive is ended by the parts: the TCAN334's
          driver dominant time-out frees the bus after 1.2 to 3.8 ms (SLLSEQ7F, tTXD_DTO, PRINTED) and the protocol forces recessive bits
          (6.3.7, p.20); but the average a supervisor's traffic sets is bounded by no row (FW-B09 bounds the rate, 1 Mbps or less, not the
          share), and T10-A2 is judged at the DECLARED 0.020 A a transceiver. OPEN DEFECT L9T5-F13: it closes when a contract row bounds each
          supervisor's transmit share so that its transceivers' average stays at or under that declaration (round 5: drafted, section 10d)
          or when the regulator holds 125 C at the bounded share (owners: Layer 5 for the row; board B's generator owner for the
          declaration). As drawn: FAILS, L9T5-F06
      (f1) one fabric faulted, its transceiver driving dominant into the fault (the 180 mA row's condition: TXD = 0 V, CANH = -12 V, RL open;
          SLLSEQ7F 5.5, p.6), the other transceiver recessive: 0.3726 A, junction 144.2 C MODEL (201.9 C MODEL as drawn). Each supervisor has a
          transceiver on each fabric, so the row applies to all three regulators. A FAULT STATE OUTSIDE T10's SCOPE (the steady demand on
          C-DEV rev 1) which the design must serve: ARCH-PCB-B-IOHA.md section 12 row 7 ('quorum continues on the other fabric') and its test
          A7. Against 125 C: FAILS (under 150 C). The driver's time-out ends a held TXD, not the controller's repeated attempts, and the current is
          under the 600 mA capability: no printed limit holds the junction at 125 C. COVERED BY CON-004 (round 5, V6-B3; round 4 read REQ-073
          and REQ-004 and missed it): REQUIREMENTS-TRACE.md, constraint, core, BLOCKER: 'the three talk over two independent CAN-FD fabrics with separate transceivers and termination.'; accepted when 'Netlist shows three independent supply branches and two fabrics; A7 (either fabric cut, the quorum holds) passes.'
          Row 7 ('One CAN fabric breaks or a transceiver fails dominant') is a failure the design must survive: 'quorum continues on the other fabric'; its test A7 'cut fabric A, then fabric B, one at a time'.
          So this state's closure is REQUIRED for CON-004. A7 as written cuts the bus (the break links): it does not exercise this row's bus
          fault, which needs its own analysis (section 10e). OPEN DEFECT L9T5-F16 (owners: Layer 5 for the firmware rows, CON-004's 'b, fw_ioctrl').
          The response drafted in round 5 (section 10f) ends the dominant drive by the firmware's schedule and a transceiver shutdown, the
          service lost stated: that supervisor silent on the faulted fabric, quorum on the other (row 7). As drawn: FAILS, L9T5-F06 as well
      (f2) both fabrics faulted, both transceivers driving into their faults: 0.5491 A, junction 176.3 C MODEL (261.4 C MODEL as drawn), on all three
          regulators. A double fault outside T10's scope (row 8, and A7's 'with both broken nothing moves'). Against 150 C, in any state: FAILS.
          The current is under the capability, so no printed limit acts, and the typical shutdown is no acceptance. COVERED BY CON-004 through
          row 8 ('Both CAN fabrics break': 'no controller can form a majority; firmware rule is that a c...') and A7's 'with both broken nothing moves': 'nothing moves' is the
          accepted outcome, but the regulators must survive it. OPEN DEFECT L9T5-F17 (owners as F16). The response must hold each regulator
          inside 150 C (or cut the transceivers' current) with the service lost stated: both fabrics silent, no majority, the voters at the
          home assignment (row 8); drafted in round 5 (section 10f). As drawn: FAILS
      the foldback (the output short's behaviour) acts in no row
   SELECTED (SESSION): K3 WITH K2's ROW AS ITS CONDITION, A DRAFTED CANDIDATE, UNCHECKED. Why: the state must be bounded under every option,
   because the controller itself has no operating point above it (section 4); with the bound the demand is 0.2291 A, which K2 alone does not hold (153.5 C)
   and K3 holds at 118.0 C on a one-resistor change in a draft this record already owns; K1 covers currents the controller cannot draw at this
   air and costs three switching stages in board B's tightest area. To reverse: drop apply_gen_sch_?_iocpre.py (the drafts are separate from
   I-03's) and take K1; it becomes necessary if Layer 5 cannot bound the state or the bench reads the LDOs hotter than the printed figure
   NOT the owner's: no requirement changes and no money beyond a resistor value. Layer 5's row is its owner's to accept (text below)
   THE ACCEPTANCE CRITERION (T10), every condition required:
   T10-A1 the contract carries a row that bounds each supervisor to VOS3 and HCLK at most 144 MHz (the least printed row over the 64 MHz it is
          taken to start at, an ASSUMPTION: RM0433 is not held; the row's owner confirms the reset state is inside the bound), with
          only the peripheral clocks the board uses (FDCAN1, FDCAN2, I2C1, the ports in use, the watchdog) enabled, and a firmware acceptance:
          the clock tree and the voltage scale read back at start and refused otherwise; the printed figure for that state with the enabled
          set's own currents summed, or the supply current measured on the first article at a junction of at least 105 C
   T10-A2 at 76.25 C inside air and that state's demand (0.2291 A here), each regulator's junction is at most 125 C on its printed thermal
          resistance (118.0 C with K3; 153.5 C as drawn), and at its declared peak 0.25 A (121.8 C with K3)
   T10-A3 each LDO's input stays over its requirement at its full 600 mA at the least set point, with the return as drawn: 3.8428 V against
          3.7693 V (+0.0735 V)
   T10-A4 the draft composes in L4-E9's order after I-03's, its divider is read in the regenerated netlist with a mutation that fails, and
          the declarations it writes are read in the intent (section 9)
   T10-A5 PHYSICAL, on the first article: each LDO's case temperature at the bounded state in a 76 C chamber, and each supervisor's supply
          current in that state; a supervisor forced out of the bound (a firmware fault) must be returned inside it by a response that acts
          before the LDO's junction reaches 150 C (the clock read-back's reset or the watchdog), the voters at their default meanwhile and the
          service lost stated; the LDO's thermal shutdown (160 C, TYPICAL, no maximum trip printed) is no acceptance: Layer 5's FMEA row
   LAYER 5'S ROW, TEXT FOR ITS OWNER (HW-FW-CONTRACT.md section 3.3, a new FW-B row; not applied by this record):
     | FW-Bxx | the supervisors' supply: a private AP2112K-3.3 each from the pre-regulated +5V_IOC (record l9t5 T10) | Run each supervisor at
       VOS3 with HCLK at most 144 MHz (no PLL above it); enable only FDCAN1, FDCAN2, I2C1, the GPIO ports in use and the IWDG; read the clock
       tree and the voltage scale back at start and stay in reset-safe outputs if they differ; never raise either at run time | record l9t5
       l9t5_t10.out (ST DS12110 Rev 10 Tables 30 and 129; Diodes DS39724 Rev. 2-2) | supply current of each supervisor in that state at a
       junction of at least 105 C; each LDO's case temperature in a 76 C chamber | OWED |

