   (2) THE CAN SERVICE UNDER THE BOUND (VOS3, at most 144 MHz, 2 % of every 100 ms per fabric)
     what the fabrics carry: the supervisors' quorum (state, leases, epochs, the votes' agreement; FW-B09, IOHA sections 6 and 7). Not on
     them: the modules' heartbeats (GPIO lines HB1 to HB3, FW-B01), the voted outputs (GPIO into the voters, FW-B12), FW-E07's stopped-fan
     report (board E's sensor controller, the kit bus, FW-E07 and V-E07's 5 s). The tree defines no CAN message rate; the record ASSUMES
     1 state frame per supervisor per fabric per 100 ms and a burst of 8 event frames in a window when ownership moves
     at  125 kbit/s:   250 dominant bit times a window:  1 frames of 135 bits (every bit counted dominant) with two acknowledgements each;
       the need 9 (NOT inside: a state frame per window only, the event burst spread over 0 windows)
     at  500 kbit/s:  1000 dominant bit times a window:  7 frames of 135 bits (every bit counted dominant) with two acknowledgements each;
       the need 9 (NOT inside: a state frame per window only, the event burst spread over 2 windows)
     at 1000 kbit/s:  2000 dominant bit times a window: 14 frames of 135 bits (every bit counted dominant) with two acknowledgements each;
       the need 9 (inside)
     REQ-004's 30 s for a moved bank (ownership plus re-enumeration) is 300 windows; at 125 kbit/s the event burst spreads over at most 8 of
     them; at 500 kbit/s and over it fits one window. A7: with one fabric cut the other carries the same traffic under the same share (the
     share is per fabric, not shared). CON-004's quorum needs two of three state frames inside the loss timeout the firmware sets: the
     share allows 7 to 14 a window at FW-B21's 500 kbit/s to 1 Mbit/s (1 at 125 kbit/s, which FW-B21 therefore excludes). So the bound keeps
     the service the tree requires, on the assumed message set (the firmware's to confirm
     against its real one, V-B21)
   (3) WHAT ENFORCES THE BOUND AND THE RESPONSE IN THE FAULTS THE CALCULATION USES (an unapplied contract row is no mechanism)
     HARDWARE, whatever the firmware does: the TCAN334's driver time-out frees a held TXD in 1.2 to 3.8 ms (PRINTED); the FDCAN's own bus-off
     sets INIT and holds TX recessive with no automatic restart (RM0433 p.2464 and p.2534; the count to bus-off is the CAN standard's, not
     held); any reset (the IWDG, started by option byte under FW-B10, on its own LSI) returns the reset state, inside the bound, with the TX
     pins high-impedance and each TXD on the transceiver's pull-up, recessive; SHDN rests on its 100 k in normal mode (the draft)
     FIRMWARE (FW-B20, FW-B21, enforceable only once applied and built): the clock and VOS read back at start; DAR = 1; the share; a fabric
     at error passive, bus-off or with no valid frame for 100 ms stopped and its transceiver shut down; probing once a second for 100 ms
     per fault, what acts and when (each window carries at most 2 ms of own dominant drive by the schedule, so a fault's current is
     averaged over the window whatever the detection time):
       B1, B2, B5 (bits read wrong)       each scheduled frame errs once (DAR); EP or BO stops the fabric and SHDN; the device's own bus-off backs it            the schedule's 2 ms a window; the stop within 100 ms
       B3, B4 (the bus still works)       nothing detects them: the share alone bounds the current, which the result above already counts                        every window
       B6 (an open: no acknowledgement)   each frame errs once (DAR, ACK errors); the no-valid-frame rule stops the fabric                                       within 100 ms; 2 ms a window before
       B7a (TXD held low)                 the DTO (hardware) frees the drive; the FDCAN errs; the firmware stops the fabric                                      3.8 ms
       B7b (driver stuck on, DTO dead)    every supervisor sees no valid frame; each stops the fabric and drives its SHDN; the failed part may ignore it (L9T5-D5) within 100 ms
     THE FIRMWARE AS THE FAULTING PARTY: a hang is ended by the IWDG (hardware-started, FW-B10) and leaves nothing new transmitted (DAR); with DAR
     mis-set and the CPU hung, B6's endless retransmission is the worst (133.2 C on rev Y, the parts-alone column), under the 150 C absolute
     maximum. A running firmware that breaks the share (a babbling supervisor) is bounded by both transceivers held dominant: 0.3666 A, 146.4 C on
     rev Y, under 150 C, over 125 C (it costs margin, not the regulator; its own fabric traffic is then the quorum's problem, an FMEA row
     IOHA section 12 does not carry: finding L9T5-F21). A running firmware that breaks the CLOCK bound (FW-B20) takes the controller
     itself outside its rating (section 4: no operating point over 200 MHz with the peripherals on) and can take the LDO over 150 C:
     nothing in hardware prevents it. That is a SYSTEMATIC firmware defect, closed by the firmware's verification (FW-B20's read-back,
     V-B20, CON-017 (4)'s firmware stage), not by a protective mechanism: an OPEN implementation obligation, not a desk-fixable circuit
     defect. Round 4's K1 (a buck per supervisor) would let the regulator survive it, but not the controller (SESSION decision L9T5-D6:
     K1 not taken for it; to reverse, round 4's K1)
   L9T5-F22's lever (SCENARIO for the T10 drafts' owner, not drafted): the LDOs' input held over its requirement at the largest current this
